WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.400
Unsupported software is difficult to defend because fixes may no longer be available.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:10.900
Payment applications should avoid relying on end-of-life frameworks, runtimes, plugins,

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.880
or server components. Dependency management starts with inventory.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.300
Teams need to know which components are used, where they run,

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:22.280
and whether they affect the cardholder data environment or connected payment workflows.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.580
Supply chain risk includes malicious packages, compromised build tools,

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.720
untrusted container images, and vulnerable third-party components.

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:34.780
Payment teams should treat build and dependency sources as part of the application

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.120
security boundary.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.900
Vulnerable libraries should be evaluated based on exposure, exploitability,

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.000
compensating controls, and business impact.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.380
Patch discipline needs enough testing support to avoid delaying critical fixes.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.480
Maintain software inventory and ownership.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.660
Use dependency scanning and vulnerability monitoring.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.200
Patch, upgrade, isolate, or replace vulnerable components promptly.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.940
Selected PCI DSS mappings for this topic include Req.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:10.600
6.3 - Security vulnerability management; Req. 11.2 - Vulnerability scanning; Req.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:15.060
6.2.4 - Address vulnerabilities promptly.
